          "id": 1,
          "question": "Differentiate precisely between the Investment Decision and the Financing Decision in Corporate Finance. Provide at least three distinct components for each decision, ensuring you address the lifecycle (not just the initial transaction) of both. Illustrate this using the Meta/WhatsApp example.",
          "marks": 10,
          "suggested_time_minutes": 15,
          "model_answer": "The Investment Decision concerns the *spending* of capital on **real assets** (e.g., CapEx, M&A), focusing on future income generation. Its scope includes: 1. Asset Acquisition (e.g., WhatsApp purchase). 2. Asset Management (ongoing utilization). 3. Asset Disposal (when utility ends).\n\nThe Financing Decision concerns the *sourcing* of capital via **financial assets** (e.g., debt, equity). Its scope includes: 1. Capital Raising (e.g., issuing shares/bonds). 2. Obligation Management (servicing interest/principal). 3. Mix Optimization (determining the optimal debt/equity ratio).\n\n**Meta Example:** The acquisition is the **Investment Decision**. Funding it via cash and shares is the **Financing Decision**. The subsequent obligation to manage the acquired entity and service any associated debt (if applicable) completes the financing lifecycle.\n\n*Mathematical Link:* $\\text{Investment Outlay} = \\text{Internal Funds} + \\text{Debt Raised} + \\text{Equity Raised}$.",
